<Base
  title="About · Andreas Jackson"
  description="About Andreas (AJ) Jackson: Berkeley Haas grad, EY analyst, founder of NotTomorrow, operator of a short-term rental business, and independent AI researcher."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose-wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Hi, I'm AJ.</h1>
      <p>
        I'm Andreas Jackson, an analyst and builder in the San Francisco Bay
        Area. I studied business at UC Berkeley's Haas School (class of 2025,
        GPA 3.7, Dean's List), spent three years running strategy engagements
        for clients like the Gates Foundation, IKEA, and T-Mobile, worked
        inside early-stage startups in London and Tokyo, and now work in EY's
        financial services assurance practice.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside the day job I like building things that have to survive contact
        with reality: <a href="/projects/nottomorrow">NotTomorrow</a>, a SaaS
        product with paying users, and
        <a href="/projects/dune-lakes-business">Dune Lakes</a>, a short-term
        rental business I help run. Most recently I ran
        <a href="/projects/scaffolding-vs-scale">an empirical study</a> on how
        AI models behave on real occupational work, which taught me more about
        research than any coursework did.
      </p>
      <p>
        What ties it together: I like owning a number. Whether it's an audit
        workpaper, a partner-scoring model, or a night's ADR, I want to know how
        it was built and be able to defend it.
      </p>

      <h2>What I care about</h2>
      <p>
        Efficiency, mostly. I'm drawn to the gap between how long something
        takes and how long it should take, which is why so much of my work ends
        up being automation: a booking export that used to take an afternoon and
        now takes seconds, or a research pipeline that reruns from one command.
        The other half of that is learning. I'd rather spend a weekend
        understanding something properly than get a faster answer I can't
        defend.
      </p>
      <p>
        A good share of my work has been for organizations trying to make
        things better: the Gates Foundation engagement, The Assistance Fund,
        and other nonprofit clients at ABA. I'd like the long arc of my career
        to leave things better than I found them, and I think the way I
        contribute is by building useful things and being rigorous about
        whether they actually work.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm looking for</h2>
      <p>
        A full-time role where analytical rigor and shipping speed both matter:
        AI implementation and applied AI, product management, data and
        analytics, strategy and operations, or strategic finance. Anywhere in
        the Bay Area works for me, San Francisco through the Peninsula and
        South Bay, and I'm also open to Santa Barbara or remote.
      </p>

      <h2>Off the clock</h2>
      <p>
        I'm competitive, and most of what I do for fun reflects that. Basketball
        and soccer, skiing, chess, and training seriously enough that health and
        fitness are a real part of how I structure a week. Video games too,
        which is how the
        <a href="/projects/deadlock-counter-buy">recommendation engine</a> on
        this site started life.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rest of it is reading and building. A weekly economics newsletter,
        the semiconductor and data-center industry (yes, I've read Chip War),
        and a lot of time experimenting with AI workflows, which you can read
        about in <a href="/how-i-work">how I work</a>.
      </p>

